/**
 * @mainpage Softloq UTF-8
 *
 * @section overview_developer Developer
 * Developed by Brandon Foster ([\@BrandonFoster](https://github.com/BrandonFoster)) under the
 * [Softloq](https://github.com/Softloq) organization.
 *
 * @section overview_version Version
 * This documentation covers **v1.0.0** of Softloq UTF-8.
 *
 * @section overview_problem The Problem
 * UTF-8's variable-length encoding means a "byte" and a "character" are not the same thing. A naive
 * implementation that reads bytes without validating them can silently accept malformed input —
 * overlong encodings, unpaired surrogates, truncated sequences, and out-of-range code points — any
 * of which can corrupt data or introduce security issues if the unvalidated bytes are trusted.
 *
 * @section overview_solution The Solution
 * Softloq UTF-8 addresses this by splitting decoding and encoding into small, independently-validated
 * layers instead of one large decoding routine:
 *
 * - `Octet` rejects byte values that can never legally appear in UTF-8 the moment they're constructed.
 * - `OctetSequence` rejects a lead octet paired with the wrong number of trailing octets before those
 *   bytes are ever interpreted as a code point.
 * - `Char` rejects code points outside the Unicode range or inside the UTF-16 surrogate range, whether
 *   they arrive as a raw code point or as a decoded octet sequence.
 *
 * Because every layer returns `std::expected<Value, Error>` instead of throwing or silently clamping
 * invalid input, callers are forced to explicitly handle malformed data at the point where it's
 * detected, rather than letting corrupted text propagate further into a program.
 *
 * @section overview_pages Further Reading
 * - @subpage usage_page
 */
